WEBVTT

00:02:57.833 --> 00:03:00.684
So let's dig into it without further ado,

00:03:00.708 --> 00:03:03.792
Danielle Allen, welcome here
to TED Connects.

00:03:05.458 --> 00:03:07.851
Danielle Allen: Thank you, Chris,
happy to be here.

00:03:07.875 --> 00:03:10.018
I'm really, really grateful
to have the chance

00:03:10.042 --> 00:03:12.476
to have this conversation with you.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:14.893
CA: It's -- It's so good,

00:03:14.917 --> 00:03:18.292
I just enjoyed our conversation
over the last couple of days.

00:03:19.375 --> 00:03:21.934
This is such a complex problem.

00:03:21.958 --> 00:03:25.018
What I kind of want you to do
is just go through it step-by-step,

00:03:25.042 --> 00:03:27.684
to see the logic

00:03:27.708 --> 00:03:30.309
of what it is that your team
are putting forward.